  "full_response": "Garbage Collection (GC) in Java and manual memory management in C++ represent two fundamentally different approaches to memory management, each with its own trade-offs. Here’s a detailed comparison:\n\n---\n\n### **1. Garbage Collection in Java**\nJava uses an **automatic garbage collector** to manage memory, relieving developers from explicitly freeing memory. The JVM handles memory allocation and deallocation.\n\n#### **How It Works:**\n- **Object Allocation**: Objects are created on the **heap** (e.g., `new Object()`).\n- **Reachability Analysis**: The GC periodically checks which objects are **reachable** (still in use) by tracing references from **GC roots** (e.g., static variables, local variables, active threads).\n- **Mark-and-Sweep (or Variants)**:\n  - **Mark Phase**: The GC marks all reachable objects.\n  - **Sweep Phase**: Unmarked (unreachable) objects are considered garbage and reclaimed.\n- **Generational GC (HotSpot JVM)**:\n  - **Young Generation (Eden, S0, S1)**: New objects are allocated here. Minor GCs clean short-lived objects frequently.\n  - **Old Generation (Tenured)**: Long-lived objects are promoted here. Major GCs (e.g., Full GC) clean this space less frequently.\n  - **Permanent Generation (Metaspace in Java 8+)**: Stores class metadata.\n- **GC Algorithms**:\n  - **Serial GC**: Single-threaded, stops application threads (STW).\n  - **Parallel GC (Throughput GC)**: Multi-threaded, optimized for throughput.\n  - **CMS (Concurrent Mark-Sweep)**: Low-latency, runs concurrently with application threads (deprecated in Java 14).\n  - **G1 (Garbage-First)**: Divides heap into regions, balances throughput and latency.\n  - **ZGC/Shenandoah**: Ultra-low-pause GCs (sub-10ms pauses).\n\n#### **Advantages:**\n- **No Manual Deallocation**: Eliminates memory leaks from forgetting to `free()`.\n- **Productivity**: Developers focus on logic, not memory management.\n- **Safety**: Prevents dangling pointers (accessing freed memory) and double frees.\n- **Dynamic Memory Handling**: Works well for long-running applications (e.g., servers).\n\n#### **Disadvantages:**\n- **Performance Overhead**: GC cycles consume CPU",
